# scripts/bench-baseline.env — the committed zsh-startup baseline and the budget CI gates on.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by scripts/bench-core.sh (#688). `--gate` — what .github/workflows/ci.yml's `bench` job
# runs — FAILS the build when the hermetic canonical-chain MEAN exceeds CORE_BENCH_BUDGET_MS;
# plain `make bench` prints the mean against CORE_BENCH_BASELINE_MS so a local run shows the
# trend. The budget lives HERE, next to the script it governs, not in workflow YAML: the
# previous 120 ms existed only in ci.yml, beside a comment guessing "~25 ms", and nothing
# recorded the real number or kept the two in step.
#
# RATCHET POLICY: BUDGET = 2 × BASELINE. Calibration, 194 `bench` job runs on ubuntu-latest
# (2026-08-26 → 2026-09-03, CORE_BENCH_RUNS=50, which hyperfine extends to ~100–240 runs):
#   run-means 11.8–31.9 ms, bimodal by runner host (fast hosts 12–18, ordinary 22–28);
#   2 of 194 above 30 ms, none above 36; σ within a run 0.3–1.8 ms, and a single 285 ms
#   outlier moved its ~200-run mean by under 1 ms.
# So 48 ms keeps 1.5× headroom over the worst run ever observed and cannot false-red on
# runner noise, while anything that DOUBLES an ordinary host's startup fails. This is a
# gross-regression gate, not an additive threshold: the compare is `mean <= budget`, and on
# a fast host (12–18 ms) the same absolute regression has more room — re-sourcing the gh/uv/ty
# completions per shell (the biggest win on record, CHANGELOG) cost +35 ms, which fails from
# an ordinary host's 24 ms and may still pass from a fast host's 12. Single forks (~2 ms) are
# invisible here by design; /shell-review and `make profile` are the tools for those.
#
# TO RE-BASELINE: sample the last ~20 green `bench` jobs on main (each log prints the mean;
# never a laptop — this is the ubuntu-latest figure). Runner hosts are BIMODAL, so ONE job is
# not a baseline: a single fast-host run (~12 ms) would set a 24 ms budget that ordinary
# 22–28 ms hosts breach with no regression at all. Take the ORDINARY-host mode (the upper
# cluster), round UP to a whole ms, set BASELINE, set BUDGET = 2 × BASELINE, and record it
# under [Unreleased] in CHANGELOG.md. scripts/test-core.sh pins the 2× rule and
# bench-core.sh --gate refuses a budget that does not exceed the baseline. Ratchet DOWN when
# the ordinary-host mode drops; never widen the budget to make a red run green.
#
# Plain KEY=VALUE, whole ms, no spaces around `=`, no trailing comment on a value line
# (tool-versions.env's shape; the reader is `sed -n 's/^KEY=//p'`).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CORE_BENCH_BASELINE_MS=24
CORE_BENCH_BUDGET_MS=48
